          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ഭർത്താവ് (bharttāvŭ) — \"husband\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit bhartṛ with a Malayalam ending, -āvŭ, fitted onto it. Inside sits the root bhṛ, 'to bear, to carry, to support', so the word says 'the one who supports'. The root is old enough to have relatives well outside India: Latin fero, 'I carry', and English bear both continue it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three of four. The last word grows from the same root."
            }
          ]
        },
